WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday. Today's video is like the ninth rib on my right side,

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.420
it comes to you in two parts.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.840
Part one: my rib.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.640
So, Hank, I broke my rib celebrating my 39th birthday last week.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.740
It's pretty uncomfortable,

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:15.560
like activities that have suddenly become extremely painful include sitting down, standing up, laughing,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.160
and also breathing.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:23.100
Also, for the record, filming youtube videos is not very comfortable. I really can't recommend any facet of the broken rib thing.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.660
This happens to be a particularly inconvenient time to have a broken rib,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.060
partly for reasons that will become clear in a couple weeks,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.680
and partly because the broken rib has coincided with a previously scheduled midlife reflection.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.300
So, I've been middle-aged for a while, in the sense that if you were to double my current age,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.780
it would be a perfectly respectable human lifespan.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.660
But I've only recently begun to FEEL middle-aged in the sense of being, you know,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.680
acutely aware that I am not as young as I used to be.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.060
If fact, up until recently, I've felt much better than I did ten or twenty years ago,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.140
largely because when I was younger,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.580
I treated my body terribly.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.540
Like, I smoked cigarettes and steadfastly refused to exercise.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.780
I remember once when I was at boarding school, I was probably about 16,

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.940
I ate nothing but nacho cheese Doritos and drank nothing but Mountain Dew for EIGHT DAYS straight.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:13.240
It was like a demented exploration of how poorly one can treat one's body before developing scurvy.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:18.320
In those days, I thought of my body as, like, a burden that I was biologically required to lug around.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.700
I thought living inside a body was the price I had to pay for consciousness,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.360
and I bought into the idea that the body wasn't just seperate from the mind, but somehow the opposite of the mind.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.080
I imagined my body as an adversary, like I was this thinking, feeling capitalized noun.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:38.520
And my body was a lowercase animal that needed to be placated so I could go on thinking and feeling.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.140
This is a seductive line of reasoning,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.980
but ultimately, a flawed one, of course, because there is no distinct line between body and mind.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:51.240
Also, when I started treating my body better - I quit smoking in 2002, ran a mile for the first time since childhood in 2005 -

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.740
I found that I got better at the activities I most associated with my mind, reading and writing.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:01.480
So anyway, having treated my body terribly when I was an adolescent and somewhat less terribly in my 30s,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.860
I've been able to feel relatively young for the last decade or so.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.480
But now that time has passed. I am old and can no longer inhale without pain

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.640
and there isn't really any moral to this story.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.480
Except, I guess, 1) your body is a temple, do not fill it with Doritos,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.840
and also, 2) do not fill it with beer on your 39th birthday, or else you will hurt yourself.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.320
Alright, moving on. Part two: the future.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.600
So, Hank, it's been almost five years since my book The Fault in our Stars was published.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.320
Lots of awesome had happened in my life in those five years.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.820
CrashCourse happened, as did The Art Assignment.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.000
Alice happened, some movies happened.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.600
But one  thing that did not happen is that I have not published a new book.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:38.020
So I have decided to kind of clear my schedule for the next several months

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.300
as I try to finish a draft of my new story,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.100
meaning that over the next ten months or so, I will only be making three public appearances, which are as follows:

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.180
1) on October 14th and 15th, I will be at NerdCon Stories in Minneapolis.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.600
While there, I'll be speaking on a few panels, recording a live edition of our podcast, and also

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.540
talking a little about mental illness and creativity.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:01.280
2) In 2017 on February 25th and 26th, I will be at NerdCon Nerdfighteria in Boston, I'm very excited about that.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.040
And 3) on May 3rd of 2017, I will be in Cleveland,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.820
giving a speech on the topic of I Don't Know What Yet Because That Seems Very Far Away.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.360
Ticket info for all those events can be found in to dooblydoo below.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.920
I'm sorry I'm not travelling more in the coming months, but I really, really, REALLY wanna finish a draft of the new book.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.860
So thanks for understanding and I hope to see lots of you in Minneapolis, Boston, or Cleveland.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.040
Hank, DFTNYBR, I will see you on Friday.

